<header class="start-masthead"><p class="docs-breadcrumb">CyberAlchemy Lab / Concepts</p><h1>Compare the concepts</h1><p class="start-deck">Use the same {{CASE_ACCOUNT}} incident to see the distinct responsibility of each concept—and the conclusions it can support.</p><p class="start-reading-time">{{READING_TIME}} minute read · Keep open beside the guide or reference library</p></header>
<section id="meaning-and-record"><h2>Meaning, format and the particular record</h2>
<p>A definition, a type contract and a record answer successive questions. Keeping them identifiable lets meaning remain stable across many records and lets formats evolve through their own governed versions.</p>
<dl class="comparison-lines"><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Definition}}">Definition</a><span>What does it mean?</span></dt><dd>“Complete export” states the identifier-coverage criterion and its scope. An accepted exact version governs that meaning.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Artifact Type}}">Artifact Type</a><span>What must this kind of record contain?</span></dt><dd>The Work contract binds meanings, canonical structure, validation and representation requirements for Work records.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Artifact}}">Artifact</a><span>Which particular recorded item?</span></dt><dd>This investigation’s Work record has its own identity, version, owner and state, under its declared type.</dd></div></dl>
<p><strong>Check the boundary:</strong> a schema-valid definition can still lack acceptance. An accepted type contract does not accept every instance that uses it. A new display or diagram does not change the underlying meaning.</p>
<p class="start-further"><a href="lab-reference-library.html#catalog-definitions">Inspect definitions</a> · <a href="lab-reference-library.html#catalog-types">Inspect record formats</a></p></section>
<section id="purpose-and-effort"><h2>Need, promised value and bounded effort</h2>
<dl class="comparison-lines"><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Intent}}">Intent</a><span>What need matters?</span></dt><dd>Customers need complete eligible data. Intent Resolution selects a response and the conditions for judging satisfaction.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Product}}">Product</a><span>What enduring value is promised?</span></dt><dd>The customer-data platform provides export capabilities. Its capability is the promised outcome; a particular Implementation realizes it.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Project}}">Project</a><span>What coordinates the effort over time?</span></dt><dd>The export-repair Project keeps its goals, decisions, evidence and work together.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Goal}}">Goal</a><span>What future result must be established?</span></dt><dd>A scoped repair outcome, with achievement criteria, required evidence and an accountable owner.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Work}}">Work</a><span>What is the bounded effort?</span></dt><dd>Investigate {{CASE_ACCOUNT}}’s output using selected inputs, constraints and expected results. Work can also exist outside a Project where its contract permits.</dd></div></dl>
<p><strong>Check the boundary:</strong> finishing an investigation does not establish that a Product promise was fulfilled or a repair Goal achieved. Those conclusions need their own criteria, evidence and transitions.</p></section>
<section id="observation-evidence-assessment"><h2>Observation, evidence and assessment</h2>
<dl class="comparison-lines"><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Operational Observation}}">Operational Observation</a><span>What was observed?</span></dt><dd>The comparison records {{CASE_MISSING_COUNT}} missing required identifiers in {{CASE_OUTPUT}}. In ordinary Project work, this is an Operational Observation; research- or experiment-specific Observation has its own boundary.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Evidence}}">Evidence</a><span>What bears on a particular claim?</span></dt><dd>The source snapshot, output and comparison support the scoped omission claim when their provenance, method and suitability can be examined. An Evidence Set groups the relevant material.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Failure Assessment}}">Failure Assessment</a><span>What conclusion is supported?</span></dt><dd>The occurrence is established in this output. Cause, wider impact and recovery remain unresolved and are evaluated independently.</dd></div></dl>
<p><strong>Check the boundary:</strong> an observation is not automatically sufficient evidence for every claim. An omission assessment does not establish a pagination defect or successful repair. A Decision chooses a response through its applicable authority.</p>
<p class="start-further"><a href="incident-walkthrough.html#evidence">Follow the scoped evidence trail →</a></p></section>
<section id="assignment-and-permission"><h2>Assignment and permission</h2>
<dl class="comparison-lines"><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Agent Assignment}}">Agent Assignment</a><span>Which participant has which job?</span></dt><dd>Assign the agent to investigate the omission as a Work item.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Delegation}}">Delegation</a><span>Which actions are permitted, and when?</span></dt><dd>Permit inspection, comparison and preparing candidate material within a recorded scope and time window.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Authority}}">Definition authority</a><span>What criterion governs this meaning?</span></dt><dd>The accepted completeness definition governs the identifier-coverage assessment within its declared scope.</dd></div></dl>
<p><strong>Check the boundary:</strong> the agent needs both an assignment and applicable permission. Knowing what “complete” means does not grant data access, deployment or incident closure authority.</p></section>
<section id="state-and-views"><h2>Saved state, selected views and session memory</h2>
<dl class="comparison-lines"><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Project State}}">Project State</a><span>What endures?</span></dt><dd>Governed external records, definitions, decisions, evidence, relationships and histories—including preserved proposals with their own standing.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Context View}}">Context View</a><span>What information answers this task request?</span></dt><dd>An immutable selection bound to the request, exact sources and declared usability conditions.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Ontology View}}">Ontology View</a><span>What connected model is selected for this purpose?</span></dt><dd>An immutable projection of an exact consistent ontology cut, with purpose, consumer, freshness, expiry and limits.</dd></div><div><dt><a href="{{DEF:Session Memory}}">Session Memory</a><span>What is temporary working memory?</span></dt><dd>Working memory belonging to one Agent Session. It expires when the session terminates.</dd></div></dl>
<p><strong>Check the boundary:</strong> views select saved state; they do not replace its canonical sources. Later machine sessions use new instances, new working memory and newly checked views and permissions.</p>
<p class="start-further"><a href="incident-walkthrough.html#handoff">See the session handoff →</a></p></section>
<section id="ontology-and-craft"><h2>Lab ontology and the Craft software model</h2>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="learning-table"><caption>Two models with different responsibilities</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Lab ontology</th><th>Craft software model</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>What is represented?</th><td>Typed project subjects and declared connections, with meanings, evidence, ownership and governance.</td><td>Software responsibilities, behavior, architecture and construction constraints.</td></tr><tr><th>What does the incident use it for?</th><td>Recover how the investigation, inputs, assessment and decisions fit together.</td><td>Express and construct the proposed software repair within supported model-to-code paths.</td></tr><tr><th>What does a graph connection establish?</th><td>Only the meaning allowed by its declared ordered endpoints and qualifiers.</td><td>The software relationship defined by Craft’s applicable model contract.</td></tr><tr><th>What requires a separate decision?</th><td>Acceptance and permitted effects on governed Lab state.</td><td>Adoption of outputs and any release or deployment through the applicable authority route.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Craft is a software-design Product within the broader Lab. A project can use Craft’s tools; code, models and checks can become Work outputs and evidence. The documentation describes how they fit together without assuming an automatic connector.</p>
<p><strong>Check the boundary:</strong> a connected ontology node is a representation of a governed subject, not a new authority source. A Craft model edge is not automatically a permitted Lab relation between two Artifact instances.</p></section>
<section id="calculation-and-operation"><h2>Calculation, Query, Rule and Operation</h2>
<dl class="comparison-lines"><div><dt>Rule<span>Does a condition hold?</span></dt><dd>Evaluates a Boolean condition.</dd></div><div><dt>Calculation<span>What value follows from the inputs?</span></dt><dd>Derives a value without changing business state. In the capacity fixture, it returns Accept or Reject.</dd></div><div><dt>Query<span>What information is retrieved?</span></dt><dd>Retrieves the selected information under its declared contract.</dd></div><div><dt>Operation<span>What business state change is requested?</span></dt><dd>Owns the state-changing behavior, such as reserving capacity. A rejected attempt can leave state unchanged.</dd></div></dl>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="learning-table"><caption>The existing Craft capacity example uses strict less-than</caption><thead><tr><th>Request / capacity</th><th>Calculation result</th><th>Supplied capacity afterward</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4 / 5</td><td>Accept</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>5 / 5</td><td>Reject</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>6 / 5</td><td>Reject</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Check the boundary:</strong> Accept is a calculated result. Reserving the available capacity belongs to a separate Operation and requires its own permitted state change. Numeric Calculations remain values, too.</p>
<p class="start-further"><a href="../craft-documentation-proposal/craft-model.html#definition">Inspect Craft’s software example →</a></p></section>
